**Here is the full airport monologue** from *The Family Man* (2000), delivered by **Nicolas Cage** as **Jack Campbell** in the final scene. He rushes to stop Kate (T\'e9a Leoni) from boarding her flight to Paris.\
\
**Jack Campbell (Airport Speech / "I Choose Us"):**\
\
> Kate! You can't go. Don't get on that plane. Please. Let's just go have a cup of coffee. That's all I'm asking for. I'm sure there's another flight to Paris tonight.  \
> \
> We have a house in Jersey. We have two kids, Annie and Josh. Annie\'92s not much of a violin player, but she tries real hard. She\'92s a little precocious, but that\'92s only because she says what\'92s on her mind. And when she smiles...  \
> \
> And Josh \'97 he has your eyes. He doesn\'92t say much, but we know he\'92s smart. He\'92s always got his eyes open. He\'92s always watching us. Sometimes you can look at him and you just know he\'92s learning something new. It\'92s like witnessing a miracle.  \
> \
> The house is a mess, but it\'92s ours \'97 after 122 more payments it\'92s gonna be ours. And you \'97 you\'92re a non-profit lawyer. That\'92s right, you\'92re completely non-profit. But that doesn\'92t seem to bother you.  \
> \
> And we\'92re in love. After 13 years of marriage we\'92re still unbelievably in love. You won\'92t even let me touch you \'92til I\'92ve said it. I sing to you. Not all the time but definitely on special occasions.  \
> \
> We\'92ve dealt with our share of surprises and made a lot of sacrifices, but we stayed together. You see, you\'92re a better person than I am. And it made me a better person to be around you.  \
> \
> I don\'92t know, maybe it was all just a dream. Maybe I went to bed one lonely night in December and I imagined it all. But I swear, nothing\'92s ever felt more real.  \
> \
> And if you get on that plane right now, it\'92ll disappear forever. I know we could both go on with our lives, and we\'92d both be fine. But I\'92ve seen what we can be like together.  \
> \
> **And I choose us.**  \
> \
> Please, Kate. One cup of coffee. You can always go to Paris. Just, please... not tonight.\
\
This is the heartfelt, desperate, and visionary plea where Jack describes the alternate family life he experienced. It\'92s widely considered one of the most romantic and emotionally charged monologues in Nicolas Cage\'92s filmography.\
