I don\'92t have to tell you things are bad. Everybody knows things are bad. It\'92s a depression. Everybody\'92s out of work or scared of losing their job. The dollar buys a nickel\'92s worth. Banks are going bust. Shopkeepers keep a gun under the counter. Punks are running wild in the street and there\'92s nobody anywhere who seems to know what to do, and there\'92s no end to it.\
\
We know the air is unfit to breathe and our food is unfit to eat. And we sit watching our TVs while some local newscaster tells us that today we had fifteen homicides and sixty-three violent crimes, as if that\'92s the way it\'92s supposed to be!\
\
We all know things are bad \'97 worse than bad \'97 they\'92re crazy. It\'92s like everything everywhere is going crazy, so we don\'92t go out anymore. We sit in the house, and slowly the world we\'92re living in is getting smaller, and all we say is: \'93Please, at least leave us alone in our living rooms. Let me have my toaster and my TV and my steel-belted radials, and I won\'92t say anything. Just leave us alone.\'94\
\
Well, I\'92m not gonna leave you alone. I want you to get mad!\
\
I don\'92t want you to protest. I don\'92t want you to riot. I don\'92t want you to write to your congressman, because I wouldn\'92t know what to tell you to write. I don\'92t know what to do about the depression and the inflation and the Russians and the crime in the street. All I know is that first, you\'92ve got to get mad.\
\
You\'92ve gotta say, \'93I\'92m a human being, goddammit! My life has value!\'94\
\
So, I want you to get up now. I want all of you to get up out of your chairs. I want you to get up right now and go to the window, open it, and stick your head out and yell:\
